# Source inputs for environments/requirements.lock
# Regenerate with:
#   python tools/update_dependencies.py --all              (full re-lock)
#   python tools/update_dependencies.py --target PACKAGE   (bump one package)
#   Not reachable via --ci-env; requirements.lock line 2 records the exact argv.
-r ../requirements.in
-r requirements-ci.in

# Security overrides - minimum versions to close CVEs that snakemake's dependency
# resolver would otherwise peg to vulnerable releases.
#
# A line here is a REQUIREMENT, not a constraint: `uv pip compile` installs
# whatever it names, whether or not anything else needs it. So a floor may only
# name a package that the -r includes above already pull in, and it must be
# deleted once they stop pulling it in. tests/test_security_floors_have_a_dependent.py
# enforces that.
#
# Until this change the spec carried four floors that broke that rule -
# cryptography, msgpack, pydantic-settings and pyjwt. Nothing in the closure of
# requirements.in + requirements-ci.in requires any of them (each lock entry was
# annotated `# via -r environments/requirements-lock.in` and nothing else), and no
# tracked module imports them. They were therefore installing seven packages
# (with cffi, pycparser and python-dotenv) into the production image, which
# installs this lock, purely to hold them at a patched version. pyjwt 2.14.0 then
# became vulnerable itself (GHSA-42vr-xj54-vc7v / CVE-2026-101918, fixed in
# 2.15.0). Deleting the four floors removes exactly those seven packages and
# changes no other pin. If a real dependency ever pulls one of them back in, the
# pip-audit job in security.yml audits this installed set and goes red on any
# unaccepted advisory once the advisory reaches pip-audit's feed (it is not a
# required check), and the floor belongs back here with that dependent named.
# aiohttp's floor moved from 3.14.3 to 3.14.4 as a routine version bump, not for an
# advisory: PyPI listed no known vulnerability for 3.14.3 on 2026-10-18.
# aiohttp: CVE-2026-54273/54274/54275/54276/54277/54278/54280, CVE-2026-50269
aiohttp>=3.14.5
# setuptools: GHSA-h35f-9h28-mq5c (MANIFEST.in exclusion bypass in sdist via
# Unicode NFC/NFD normalization collision on macOS APFS/HFS+).
# This floor used to conflict with torch 2.12.0's `setuptools<82` build-metadata
# cap, which made the spec unsatisfiable for any resolving installer
# (ResolutionImpossible) and forced every recompile through a uv override file.
# torch 2.13.0 raised that cap to `setuptools>=77.0.3`, so the documented exit
# condition is met: overrides.txt has been retired and this spec now resolves
# normally. The floor itself stays - it is the security constraint, not the
# workaround.
setuptools>=84.1.0
# gitpython: PYSEC-2026-3785, PYSEC-2026-3786, PYSEC-2026-3787, PYSEC-2026-3788, CVE-2026-78679
# gitpython: GHSA-83vg-56qc-22m7 / CVE-2026-114145 (high; per its title, GitPython
# validates a submodule's ".git" path only after cloning into it). It affects
# <= 3.2.0 and lists 3.2.1 as the fix. Pulled in by snakemake.
gitpython>=3.2.1
# urllib3: CVE-2026-97687 / GHSA-8988-9cw3-xx77 (HTTPS-proxy and target TLS
# settings), CVE-2026-97688 / GHSA-gh4c-6fx4-qh6g and CVE-2026-97689 /
# GHSA-vxq7-64xx-v4gw (streaming decompression and chunked decoding). All three
# list 2.8.0 as the fix.
urllib3>=2.8.0
# certifi: GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2 / PYSEC-2026-4320 (critical; releases before
# 2026.10.18 bundle the emSign ECC Root CA - G3 root, and eight intermediate CA
# certificates that chain to it have compromised private keys). It lists
# 2026.10.19 as the fix. Pulled in by requests.
certifi>=2026.10.19
# pillow: CVE-2026-112403 / GHSA-8g5g-8vmr-9782 (critical; heap buffer overflow in
# ImagingJpegDecode), six high (CVE-2026-112397, 112400, 112404, 112895, 112896,
# 112897) and two medium (CVE-2026-112395, 112899). All nine list 13.0.0 as the
